      "note": "אֲשֶׁר ... שָׁמָּה is the relative-plus-resumptive-adverb idiom English fuses into the single word 'whither'. The corpus is 6-2 for one unit headed on the relative: 1KI.21.18 and 1KI.8.47 'whither', 1KI.17.19, 1KI.4.28 and 1KI.7.7 'where', 1KI.8.21 'wherein is' — and 1KI.8.47 and 1KI.21.18 fuse it across an intervening verb, so rule 35's distance clause covers this shape. 1KI.18.10 and 1KI.7.8 instead leave שָׁם unrepresented under rule 22, but rule 30's closing sentence puts rule 22 on a repetition of the same element rather than the parts of an idiom, and a relative plus a locative are not the same element. The directional he joins its host, as every Sd in the corpus does and as JOL.3.11's שָׁמָּה → 'thither' does here.",
